HTTP(1)                      User Commands

NAME
    http - fetch a URL with HTTP/1.0

SYNOPSIS
    http [-v] [-t SECONDS] [-o FILE] http[s]://HOST[:PORT]/PATH

DESCRIPTION
    Sends one GET request and writes the response body to standard output
    or FILE. -v prints the status line and headers to standard error. -t
    bounds the connection and every wait for data to SECONDS, 30 by
    default; 0 waits without a bound. When the server sends
    Content-Length, a body that ends early or runs longer is an error,
    and a failed transfer removes FILE instead of leaving part of it. The
    exit status is 0 for a 2xx response, 1 for any other status or a
    failed connection or transfer, 2 for a bad URL. An https:// URL uses
    TLS 1.3. The certificate of the server must lead to a certificate of
    /etc/ssl/cert.pem, or of the file in the environment variable
    SSL_CERT_FILE, and must be valid for the host. Servers without TLS 1.3
    are refused. There is no redirect following and no chunked decoding
    beyond what an HTTP/1.0 server sends with Connection: close. pkg(1)
    uses the same client.

SEE ALSO
    nc(1), pkg(1), index(7)
